{"author":{"id":"a51b5961cc6de343a64125d8eb6a0f965aa434020b9c46ed932a1bbf143eb532"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1791585050,"nonce":"65k7xUE8CmFP/nDtRomtO6Q0v98=","message":"Run-time CSS and compile-time CSS landed in b6d4a30 and 7b95871 (blinc_abi 0c0bf0c). CI pins 0c0bf0c and is green.\n\n**Binding (b6d4a30)**: one css::styled::Styles per NativeLayout. Layout.addStyleSheet(text | compiled bytes, {at, file}) with diagnostics, removeStyleSheet, setTheme, setColorScheme, setRootFontSize; the viewport is the compute size. Restyle runs in Layout.compute before the native layout; onRestyle gets the paint and text declarations of nodes whose changed (plus overflow, so the host knows what scrolls), and the errors. Tree edits tell the cascade what moved (insert, detach, remove, setChildren), and removed nodes are forgotten.\n\n**Host**: every element and text node is described to the cascade through the command buffer (ELEMENT, STATES commands): tag, id, classes, attributes, inline declarations, and states from the input layer (hover, active, focus, focus-visible, focus-within, disabled, enabled). Text nodes are anonymous elements of type text, so color and font properties inherit natively; the JavaScript text inheritance is gone. setProperty writes inline declarations (numbers become px for length properties); Brush and channel values still set paint directly over the cascade. Invalid values are reported through host.onStyleErrors rather than thrown. Names cross as atoms of the context's table (Layout.intern keeps a Map from string to atom; one cssIntern call per batch of new names): that is ede8ea0's interning for class, attribute, tag and property names.\n\n**One parser**: LayoutNode.setLayoutProperty and the typed LayoutStyle's box-model fields go through css::layout::layout_writes natively (CSS_PROPERTY command); the TypeScript name-to-id parser (layoutDeclaration, layoutPropertyNames) is removed. The router-id API (LayoutProperty, setProperty(id)) stays.\n\n**Vite (7b95871)**: blincCss() compiles .css imports with the addon (imports read relative to the importing file and watched), exports the bytes and frozen classes, vars (name to --name) and keyframes, writes card.d.css.ts, and fails the build with the error at file:line:column. compileCss() is exported for tools.\n\n**Tests**: tests/native-css.mjs renders one scene styled by a sheet (classes, :first-child, var() with a theme fallback, em/rem, a media query) and the same scene set by explicit properties: identical geometry and identical pixels; compiled bytes give the same pixels; hover restyles; a theme variable recolours; a narrow viewport triggers the media rule; removing the sheet unsets it; diagnostics carry positions. The adapters test adds a class-styled scene for the JSX runtime and the Vue renderer: identical pixels, and a class change (Vue's reactive class binding) restyles. tests/native-vite-css.mjs builds an import, checks the exports and the run-time bytes, runs tsc with allowArbitraryExtensions (classes.titel is TS2551 \"Did you mean title?\"), and checks a broken sheet fails the build at its line.\n\n**Cost** (2026-10-09, Node 24.2.0, M-series Mac, release addons, load average about 9; benchmarks/host-frame.mjs, 6,031 nodes, 300 paint and 300 text edits and 30 moves a frame; old is 123f1f5 built in a worktree, run alternately; median ms):\n- old 94.2 and 70.6; new 125.5 and 88.4. Native calls 2 to 3 a frame (the restyle).\n- Per frame, cssRestyle takes 20-28 ms; compute is unchanged (107 vs 108, 83 vs 80). blinc_abi 0c0bf0c already stops unchanged router writes from dirtying layout.\n- Why: each edited cell's inline change marks it changed, and Styles restyles a changed node's subtree and every later sibling's, so 300 edits spread over 30 rows restyle nearly the whole tree each frame. An inline-only or state-only change cannot change which selectors match other nodes; restyling the node and its inheriting subtree would do. That is in css::styled, so I left it for the cascade's owner.\n\n**Left here**: paint still parsed in TypeScript from resolved declarations (routing paint natively is the next step); optional utility classes; the rules export and css({...}) extraction from the design note.","files":null}]}